      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Two lime‑kilns operated in the early 1900s, one owned by the Hartman family and the other by the Blair Limestone Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dcnr/recreation/where-to-go/state-parks/find-a-park/canoe-creek-state-park/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Blair Limestone Company kilns were a subsidiary of Jones and Laughlin Steel, feeding lime to Pittsburgh’s steel mills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        19,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.pa.gov/agencies/dcnr/recreation/where-to-go/state-parks/find-a-park/canoe-creek-state-park/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A short Limestone Trail hike from the park’s beach leads past the abandoned kilns and a historic railroad spur.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
